ONE FAILING CELL, AND THIS TIME IT IS REAL. I ran it rather than trusting the label, having
been wrong twice this week by not doing so. glm's `the_same_arm_name_on_two_witnesses_stays_
two_witnesses` against or-nemotron:

    left: [("t", "a"), ("u", "a")]   right: [("u", "a")]

Two witnesses both named "a", one failing {t}, the other failing {u}. Clause 7 says
`unique_failures` returns a test name "exactly when exactly one witness lists it". Each of t
and u is listed by exactly one witness, so both are unique failures. or-nemotron collapses
same-named witnesses before counting, which the spec never asks for, and loses one. Traceable
to a clause the spec pins by number -- a real defect, not an unpinned boundary.

Which matters, because or-nemotron wrote 37 tests, more than anyone in the field, and metrics
alone would have handed it the task on TestDepth by a margin of 14. It has the deepest suite
and a confirmed defect. Those are not in tension: a suite cannot catch what its author did not
think of.

THE SHARPEST CRITIQUE FOUND TWO CONTRADICTIONS IN MY OWN SPEC. gemini on glm:

  "The specification contains a direct contradiction between the 'Reuse existing types'
   directive ... and two stricter gate constraints: the task's single-file modification
   boundary, and the verbatim 'Exact API' fixing `pub enum Verdict` with five distinct
   variants. Because `gate::Verdict` is a closed 8-variant enum ... it cannot express
   witness-level matrix semantics."

Correct. The rubric says a new public type whose name already exists in the crate is a defect;
the Exact API orders one; the scope gate makes extending `gate::Verdict` an `OutOfScope`
failure. Three rules, no legal move. Every arm had to break one.

  "The variant documentation suggests `NoEvidence` applies whenever no test names were
   recorded, but Clause 6 explicitly commands that non-empty slices with empty `failed` sets
   yield `Clean`."

Also correct, and also mine: the doc comment I wrote for the variant and the clause I wrote for
the boundary say different things. Both arms resolved it the same way -- `NoEvidence` only for
an empty slice -- and both documented the call-site contract. Filed.

or-nemotron on gemini is six findings, all real and all cosmetic: an unreachable `else`, a
redundant emptiness check after dedup, a `dedup()` on a BTreeMap-derived vector that cannot
have duplicates, and an `Occurrence` struct storing one arm name while relying on a later
`count == 1` filter to keep it correct. Nothing behavioural. Worth having; not worth a task.

DECISION. gemini on survival -- the only arm to survive the field's one discriminating suite --
on 28 in-spec tests, and on the critique that found two contradictions in the specification
rather than in a rival. glm's suite is the better instrument here and caught the defect; its
implementation simply never met a foreign discriminating suite to be tested by.

